{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791312436,"nonce":"OVeE93YorF8LO4Ms4VoHZpCQBow=","message":"Decision: keep this issue open; no inliner change landed. The working tree and isolated NUC source are restored to 57b6a9167470ed4cbc796d4e2ba40282a1a12e7b. The allocator improvement in that commit remains independent of this experiment.\n\nThe \u003c=24-instruction scalar-only dispatch exception, when also used for outside-loop eligibility, reintroduced sparsematmult's warmed ~61 ms iterations (standard wall 576 -\u003e 603 ms). Its trace proves the wrapper now DOES inline, including outside loops. Separating the two decisions fixes this: checked wrappers retain the existing outside-loop cost, while the bounded dispatch exception permits their hot-loop sites. Return joins must recognize a fallback result passed through a phi after nested inlining.\n\nFinal bounded variant, quiet NUC, LLVM verified, ordinary interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM tiering, CPUs 0-7, alternating processes and independent answers:\n- sparsematmult standard counts: wall 572.599 -\u003e 550.687 ms, mean 75.587 -\u003e 72.041 ms.\n- FFT standard counts: wall 1225.732 -\u003e 1181.621 ms, mean 207.403 -\u003e 197.699 ms.\n- SOR fast counts: wall 8052.963 -\u003e 6978.470 ms, kernel 7859.164 -\u003e 6785.037 ms.\n- All 17 Lua benchmark answers pass in five-round comparison; initial operator wall 212.292 -\u003e 234.968 ms and tokenize 194.148 -\u003e 199.326 ms.\n- Nine-round repeat: operator reverses to 225.016 -\u003e 213.245 ms, so the apparent large operator regression is NOT confirmed. nbody 192.684 -\u003e 193.932 and trees 204.210 -\u003e 202.346 are small changes. Tokenize still regresses: 193.507 -\u003e 199.856 ms (kernel 156.644 -\u003e 161.234), consistent with prior variants.\n\nDo not present the inliner gains as committed performance. Whole-body callee pricing still trades off performance across workloads; a narrower call-site treatment or specialization needs separate evidence. The original blanket scalar exception was rejected for a clear sparsematmult warm regression, while broad cold exclusion was rejected for recurring Lua costs. The final candidate repairs the sparse regression but still increases tokenize wall time by about 3% in the repeat.\n\nValidation of the held final source: 19 inliner tests, including fast/fallback/cold-path interpreter execution, return-phi handling, cold-body size limits and checked-wrapper outside-loop rejection. Four implicit-aggregate-return tests cover interpreter, Cranelift and LLVM. Local LLVM conformance passes 43 Lua and 50 Python groups. Final candidate NUC checks cover the three targeted Python kernels and all 17 Lua kernels; the earlier broad-cold variant was tested against all 22 supported Python checks. Full official Lua conformance was run for the COMMITTED allocator, not this held inliner source.\n\nSaved patch: /tmp/zyntax-scalar-adapter-held-final.patch (local). Earlier variants: /tmp/zyntax-scalar-adapter-cold.patch and /tmp/zyntax-scalar-adapter-bounded.patch. Local and NUC JSON: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-scalar-adapter-final-{speed-standard,speed-ab,lua-ab,lua-repeat}.json. Bounded sparse paired traces/HIR: /tmp/zyntax-scalar-adapter-bounded-sparse-{before,after}.trace and corresponding -hir directories on the NUC. Source restore rebuild logs: /tmp/zyntax-nuc-restore-57b6a916.log and /tmp/zyntax-local-restore-57b6a916.log.","files":null}]}